erType":"CIVIL","userId":"33399b6894184192ba57d187ad8af09a"},{"sequence":8,"submissionId":20210023718,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Stefan Bieniawski"},"organization":{"name":"Blue Origin, LLC"}},"id":"2478c6bd30b94542a803f77b42282dc0","userType":"OTHER"},{"sequence":9,"submissionId":20210023718,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Lloyd Strohl, III"},"organization":{"name":"Blue Origin, LLC"}},"id":"4bca68a7c10e4d238845c00298c0ffcb","userType":"OTHER"},{"sequence":10,"submissionId":20210023718,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Andrew Berning Jr. "},"organization":{"name":"Blue Origin, LLC"}},"id":"702228ae90204721b1c34dbea19c4219","userType":"OTHER"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Conference Paper","technicalReviewType":"NASA_PEER_COMMITTEE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20210023718,"sourceIdentifiers":[{"number":"https://arc.aiaa.org/doi/pdf/10.2514/6.2021-0256","submissionId":20210023718,"id":"cf0cee07c7094fb4b290f07d9c2e1c5a","descriptor":"SPLICE project overview","type":"URL"},{"number":"https://sites.utexas.edu/renato/files/2018/05/UDU_Information.pdf","submissionId":20210023718,"id":"3d52621793ca47f782bceead52c5c46c","descriptor":"UDU formulation","type":"URL"},{"number":"https://arc.aiaa.org/doi/10.2514/6.2020-0844","submissionId":20210023718,"id":"2a6be94b133d46cbb0a4a4be9e7e094e","descriptor":"DQG formulation","type":"URL"},{"number":"https://arc.aiaa.org/doi/10.2514/6.2020-0367","submissionId":20210023718,"id":"7b6362aff4d343128654bd9d79bcba08","descriptor":"Flight HW","type":"URL"},{"number":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZUZZ0EDzII0","submissionId":20210023718,"id":"69def93854084e33a7aae7dcdf14ea79","descriptor":"YouTube video of launch, altitude, altitude rate displayed on video","type":"URL"}],"created":"2021-11-01T17:09:45.0502770+00:00","center":{"code":"JSC","name":"Johnson Space Center","id":"dd46994b91a94cb2a2722617cbbadc55"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"There is currently renewed interest in robotic and crewed landers for a return to the lunar surface. Advanced guidance and navigation algorithms are essential to accurately delivering cargo and crew safely to the moon successfully. This paper reports the overall performance of an integrated set of navigation and guidance algorithms flown on a terrestrial suborbital rocket up to an altitude of approximately 100km. The navigation algorithm consists of an onboard extended Kalman Filter (EKF) that ingests multiple sensor measurements, one of which is the output from a terrain relative navigation (TRN) algorithm that cross-references camera images to on-board satellite imagery to perform feature correlation within the camera image. The guidance algorithm solves for a 6-degree-of-freedom (DoF) optimal trajectory using a successive convexification method during powered descent. The altitude range as well as the landing dynamics experienced during this test flight are realistic for an extraterrestrial landing and provide an invaluable data set to gauge the current development of these landing algorithms in an effort to advance the overall software readiness levels (SRL). This paper will delve into different aspects of each algorithm and present an analysis of the in-flight performance of the algorithms. This flight was conducted under the National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) Safe and Precise Landing Integrated Capabilities Evolution (SPLICE) project focused on technology advancement for landing applications. ","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","cui":{"isCui":false,"submissionId":20210023718,"id":"dae083aa71ad43b4ab8cc30eba774cb9"},"meetings":[{"country":"US","submissionId":20210023718,"endDate":"2022-01-07T06:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"organizationId":"71fb4af96d4a59b5a0a2084db7b4ce35","meta":{"organization":{"name":"American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics","location":"Reston, Virginia, United States"}},"meetingId":"db638d9687e842e790729c8f8f761afc","id":"e04a4e81e35a40959a5b8fd9fb84c9b2"}],"name":"AIAA SciTech Forum","location":"San Diego, CA","id":"db638d9687e842e790729c8f8f761afc","url":"https://www.aiaa.org/SciTech","startDate":"2022-01-03T06:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"AIAA_SciTech2022_TP2_NAVDQG.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20210023718/downloads/AIAA_SciTech2022_TP2_NAVDQG.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20210023718/downloads/AIAA_SciTech2022_TP2_NAVDQG.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20210023718/downloads/AIAA_SciTech2022_TP2_NAVDQG.pdf.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-10-29-05-27"}